cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Syntonic Engine: one formula, three facts about the machine, no tuning"
abstract: >-
  One formula, three facts about the machine, no tuning: the engine sets the time window of a correction or an
  estimate from statistics measured in the signal, by a law derived from first principles (Theorem U' of the Syntony
  Principle); its only machine-specific inputs are three facts measured once: the loop timing and delay, the
  actuator's authority in a correction loop (which since 0.2.8 also sets the correction limit) or the fast sensor's
  drift in an estimator, and access to the read noise. The law sets a window, not a loop gain; stability stays with
  the machine. Two options, off by default since 0.2.9, add a servo lead on the planned velocity and let the window
  travel in 1/tau (the e-geodesic of Dual Geodesy). Implemented in Python, C++ and Rust, which agree to 2.5e-13 rad.
  On synthetic benches it is at parity with hardened Bayesian Kalman banks within its class and behind them where a
  second-order model applies; on the simulated MAESTRO 1 joint bench, against a tuned PID given the same correction
  limit, its baton-tip error under steady noise is at parity on one excerpt and 20 % higher on the other, and 17 to
  31 % lower when the noise jumps, but given the same servo lead and faster frames and re-tuned for them the PID's
  error is 29 % lower under steady noise and level when the noise jumps; its larger joint errors cancel at the tip
  because they carry the gesture's planned acceleration, seen through the lightly damped loop the correction forms
  with the servo; against the best fixed window its lead grows with the size of the change in conditions, from about
  4.5 % to about 15 % when the read noise jumps a hundredfold; in a one-axis simulated flight controller on the
  X500's logged noise, its attitude estimator, with nothing tuned against truth, divides the error of the flown
  fixed window by 8.6 and is 18 % behind a Kalman filter tuned against truth; in a simulated quadrotor attitude
  estimator, with the gyro's drift measured once at rest, it lands within a few percent of the best fixed window
  under moderate changes of the vibration (it overshoots it when the vibration rises x256), at parity with a tuned
  Kalman filter under steady vibration, and beats a fixed-noise Kalman filter when the vibration rises; in
  simulation, on gyro drifts drawn from the laws the theory assumes, the optimal exponent from nominal to x256
  vibration follows 1/(2H+1) across H for a fractional drift (its gain in error over the square root resolved at H =
  0.9, not at 0.75), and the law's local form, fed the drift's structure function measured once at rest, predicts
  the optimal window and its exponent through crossovers of the drift and a jump of the optimum, from predictions
  frozen before the evaluation; on logs of a real quadrotor on the ground, the measured noise and drift matrices
  predict that estimating roll and pitch jointly, as an EKF does, would gain under 0.5 % (RMS) over the law axis by
  axis. The law has not yet run as an estimator on hardware. The hypothesis under test is portability across
  machines of different physics and, for the law, the living.
